From e8c97aa288cf3f0ca05fb35f741ea5b5d92f652f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 21:08:51 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 107/107] Ad hoc server: a public server, and copies kept apart
 by version

The ad hoc server was written for friends who trust each other. A port
that runs one for strangers (a public lobby, always on) needs it to trust
its players less, and to keep apart copies that cannot play together.

Versions:
- A login may carry a version tag, after the nickname's terminating NUL
  in the nickname field ("v=", the tag, NUL; protocol.hpp). A server that
  knows nothing of it reads the nickname as before, so a tagged copy logs
  in anywhere.
- GameProfile::adhoc_version gives the tag (1 to 48 letters, digits and
  . _ - +); the client sends it (Identity::version).
- The server keeps players of different tags apart: groups, scans,
  broadcasts and chat. ServerPlayer::version reports it.

ServerConfig::public_server:
- Player ids are numbers, never the player's IPv4 address.
- A MAC stays with the first connection that logged in with it: a login
  with it from another address is refused while that connection has been
  heard from in the last 10 s (kMacTakeoverQuietMs); from the same address
  it replaces the older one, as a reconnect does.
- A relay connection must name the MAC of a player logged in from its
  address (it waits up to 2 s for a login it overtook).
- max_logins_per_address and max_relays_per_address, and
  bytes_per_second_per_address: an address over its rate is not read
  until it has some back, so it waits as on a slow link.

Whatever the kind: max_logins and max_relays replace the fixed limits;
refuse_address and refuse_mac turn a player away at connection or login
and drop one online within a second; ServerStatus::refused counts them.

Tested: adhoc_tests --loopback (MSVC, Windows) with a new public_server
check (21 checks: the tag in a login, versions apart, the relay tied to
the login, the rate, the per-address limit, both refusals), and the
existing loopback checks, all passing. A standalone server built on this
by a port, on Windows (MSVC) and Linux (GCC 13), passed a scripted run
on loopback. Not run: the all-interface ad hoc suite (it raises a
firewall prompt), a server on a real network, many players.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
